-- Q1: easy / L1 — HTM amortization method requirement
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Under ASC 320, which method must be used to amortize premiums and discounts on held-to-maturity debt securities?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Effective interest method", "Straight-line method", "Sum-of-the-years-digits method", "Declining balance method"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 320-10-35-1, held-to-maturity debt securities must use the effective interest method to amortize premiums and discounts, ensuring interest income reflects a constant yield. Wrong (B): Straight-line is permitted only when results are not materially different from the effective interest method and is not the required method. Wrong (C): Sum-of-the-years-digits is a depreciation method for tangible assets, not applicable to debt securities. Wrong (D): Declining balance is an accelerated depreciation method, not used for investment amortization.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — AFS unrealized gain effect on financial statements
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Whitmore Industries holds an available-for-sale debt security purchased for $92,000. At year-end, its fair value is $98,500. What is the most likely effect on Whitmore's financial statements?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Net income increases by $6,500", "Other comprehensive income increases by $6,500 with no effect on net income", "Retained earnings increases directly by $6,500", "No recognition until the security is sold"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 320-10-35-1, unrealized gains on available-for-sale debt securities are reported in other comprehensive income, not net income. The $6,500 gain ($98,500 − $92,000) increases accumulated OCI on the balance sheet. Wrong (A): Reporting unrealized gains in net income applies to trading securities, not AFS debt securities. Wrong (C): OCI flows through comprehensive income, not directly to retained earnings. Wrong (D): AFS securities are remeasured to fair value each period; recognition is not deferred until sale.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Debt vs equity security classification
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Under ASC 320, which feature distinguishes a debt security from an equity security for classification purposes?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The security pays periodic dividends", "The security has a determinable maturity date", "The security represents an ownership interest", "The security is traded on a public exchange"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 320-10-20, equity securities represent ownership interests such as common or preferred stock, while debt securities represent creditor relationships with contractual cash flows. The distinguishing feature of an equity security is its ownership interest. Wrong (A): Dividends are associated with equity securities, not debt securities; this does not describe debt. Wrong (B): A determinable maturity date is a characteristic of debt securities, not equity securities. Wrong (D): Both debt and equity securities may be publicly traded; exchange listing does not distinguish the two.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Significant influence threshold for equity method
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Under ASC 323, what ownership percentage generally establishes a presumption of significant influence requiring the equity method?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["10% or more", "Greater than 50%", "Less than 20%", "20% to 50%"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 323-10-15-6, ownership of 20% to 50% of voting stock creates a rebuttable presumption that the investor has significant influence over the investee, requiring the equity method. Wrong (A): A 10% threshold has no special significance for equity method classification under ASC 323. Wrong (B): Greater than 50% ownership generally requires consolidation under ASC 810, not merely the equity method. Wrong (C): Below 20% ownership generally presumes no significant influence, and the investment is accounted for under ASC 321.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Equity method differential amortization effect
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Grandview Corp. acquired 30% of Fenwick Inc. for $1,200,000 when Fenwick's book value was $3,500,000. The excess is attributable to equipment with a 10-year remaining life. What is the effect of the differential on Grandview's equity method income each year?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Annual equity income is reduced by $15,000", "Annual equity income is increased by $15,000", "No effect because the differential is amortized only on the investee's books", "Annual equity income is reduced by $150,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Grandview paid $1,200,000 for 30% of $3,500,000 book value ($1,050,000), creating a $150,000 excess attributable to equipment. Under ASC 323-10-35-13, this differential is amortized over 10 years ($150,000 ÷ 10 = $15,000), reducing equity method income annually. Wrong (B): Differential amortization reduces, not increases, the investor's share of investee income. Wrong (C): The investor must amortize the differential on its own books; the investee makes no adjustment. Wrong (D): $150,000 is the total differential, not the annual amortization amount.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: easy / L2 — Debt security premium amortization calculation
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Linden Financial purchased a held-to-maturity bond on January 1, 2026, for $103,500 with a face value of $100,000, a 6% stated rate, and a 5% market yield. What is the approximate interest income for the first year?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$6,000", "$5,175", "$5,000", "$3,500"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 320-10-35-1, interest income on HTM securities is calculated using the effective interest method: $103,500 × 5% = $5,175. The difference between the $6,000 cash received and $5,175 income reduces the premium. Wrong (A): $6,000 is the cash coupon received (face × stated rate), not the interest income under the effective interest method. Wrong (C): $5,000 applies the market rate to face value rather than the carrying amount. Wrong (D): $3,500 is the premium amount, not interest income.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q7: hard / L3 — Transfer from AFS to HTM treatment of unrealized gain
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Redfern Capital transfers a debt security from available-for-sale to held-to-maturity on March 1, 2026, when its amortized cost is $88,000, fair value is $91,400, and accumulated OCI includes a $3,400 unrealized gain. How does the transfer affect Redfern's financial statements?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The $3,400 gain is recognized immediately in net income", "The security is recorded at $88,000 with no OCI adjustment", "The security is recorded at $91,400 and the $3,400 in AOCI is amortized as a yield adjustment over the remaining life", "The $3,400 is reclassified from AOCI directly to retained earnings"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 320-10-35-10, when a debt security is transferred from AFS to HTM, it is recorded at fair value ($91,400). The unrealized gain in AOCI ($3,400) is not removed immediately but is amortized over the remaining life as a yield adjustment, offsetting the premium. Wrong (A): The unrealized gain is not recognized in income upon transfer; it remains in AOCI and amortizes. Wrong (B): The security transfers at fair value, not amortized cost, and the AOCI balance is not ignored. Wrong (D): AOCI is amortized systematically, not reclassified in a lump sum to retained earnings.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Equity method investee OCI proportional share
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Archer Holdings owns 40% of Sable Corp. During 2026, Sable reports net income of $600,000 and an unrealized loss on AFS securities of $80,000 in OCI. How should Archer reflect Sable's OCI item?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Ignore the OCI item because it does not affect net income", "Include $32,000 as a reduction to equity method investment income", "Report $80,000 loss in Archer's OCI", "Report $32,000 loss in Archer's other comprehensive income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 323-10-35-18, an investor recognizes its proportionate share of the investee's OCI items in its own OCI. Archer records 40% × $80,000 = $32,000 as a loss in OCI and adjusts the investment account. Wrong (A): The investor must recognize its share of investee OCI; ignoring it violates the equity method. Wrong (B): OCI items are reported in the investor's OCI, not as adjustments to equity method income on the income statement. Wrong (C): Archer records only its 40% share ($32,000), not the full $80,000.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — Fair value option vs equity method evaluation
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Meridian Partners acquires 25% of Solstice Inc. for $2,800,000. Solstice is a volatile biotech firm with significant R&D spending that depresses short-term earnings. Meridian's portfolio strategy emphasizes reporting investments at current market values for investor transparency. Under ASC 825, Meridian is considering electing the fair value option instead of the equity method. Which factor most strongly supports this election?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The fair value option, once elected under ASC 825-10-25-1, reports changes in fair value through net income, aligning with Meridian's transparency objective", "The fair value option eliminates the need for any disclosure about the investee", "The equity method produces higher reported income during R&D-heavy periods", "The fair value option can be revoked if Solstice becomes profitable"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 825-10-25-1, the fair value option for equity method investments reports all fair value changes in net income, providing the market-value transparency Meridian seeks. This is the strongest supporting factor. Wrong (B): ASC 825-10-50 requires extensive disclosures even when the fair value option is elected; it does not eliminate disclosure requirements. Wrong (C): During R&D-heavy periods with depressed earnings, the equity method would report lower income from the investee's losses, not higher income. Wrong (D): Under ASC 825-10-25-4, the fair value option election is irrevocable once made; it cannot be changed later.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Variable interest entity primary beneficiary
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Northbridge Corp. holds a 15% equity interest in Cypress Ventures LLC, a variable interest entity. Northbridge has the power to direct activities that most significantly impact Cypress's economic performance and absorbs 60% of expected losses through a credit guarantee. Eastfield Partners holds 35% equity but has no decision-making authority. Under ASC 810-10-25, which entity should consolidate Cypress?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Neither entity because no single party owns more than 50%", "Northbridge, because it meets both criteria for primary beneficiary", "Eastfield, because it holds the largest equity interest", "The entity should remain unconsolidated as a joint venture"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 810-10-25-38A, the primary beneficiary of a VIE must have both (1) power to direct activities most significantly affecting economic performance and (2) obligation to absorb losses or right to receive benefits that could be significant. Northbridge satisfies both criteria. Wrong (A): VIE consolidation does not depend on majority ownership; it depends on power and economics. Wrong (C): Eastfield's larger equity stake is irrelevant because it lacks decision-making power over significant activities. Wrong (D): VIE guidance requires consolidation by the primary beneficiary; an unconsolidated treatment is not an option when a primary beneficiary exists.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Equity method income with amortizable differential
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Kessler Group owns 25% of Brixton Technologies, acquired for $950,000 when Brixton's net assets had a book value of $3,200,000. The $150,000 excess is attributable to a patent with a 15-year remaining life. Brixton reports 2026 net income of $480,000. What is Kessler's equity method investment income for 2026?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$120,000", "$130,000", "$110,000", "$150,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Kessler's share of Brixton's income is 25% × $480,000 = $120,000. The patent differential amortization is $150,000 ÷ 15 = $10,000 per year, reducing equity income to $120,000 − $10,000 = $110,000. Under ASC 323-10-35-13, differentials attributable to finite-lived assets must be amortized. Wrong (A): $120,000 omits the required differential amortization of $10,000. Wrong (B): $130,000 incorrectly adds the amortization instead of subtracting it. Wrong (D): $150,000 has no basis in the calculation and confuses the total differential with annual income.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — AFS credit loss vs non-credit component
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Harmon Savings holds an AFS debt security with an amortized cost of $200,000 and a fair value of $178,000. Harmon determines that $14,000 of the decline is attributable to credit deterioration. Under ASC 326-30, what amounts are recognized?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$22,000 allowance for credit losses charged to net income", "$22,000 loss recognized entirely in OCI", "$14,000 credit loss in net income; $8,000 non-credit loss in OCI", "$14,000 allowance for credit losses charged to net income; $8,000 non-credit loss in OCI"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 326-30-35-3, for AFS debt securities, the credit-related portion ($14,000) is recognized as an allowance for credit losses through net income. The non-credit portion ($22,000 − $14,000 = $8,000) is recognized in OCI. Wrong (A): The entire $22,000 decline is not all credit-related; only $14,000 was attributed to credit deterioration. Wrong (B): Recognizing the full amount in OCI ignores the credit component that must be charged to income. Wrong (C): This answer identifies the correct amounts but omits that the credit portion is recorded as an allowance, not a direct write-down.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — Step acquisition from fair value to equity method
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Piedmont Corp. initially held a 10% interest in Thornbury Ltd. accounted for at fair value under ASC 321, with a carrying amount of $180,000 (original cost $150,000). Piedmont then acquires an additional 15% for $400,000, bringing ownership to 25% and establishing significant influence. The total fair value of the 25% interest on the acquisition date is $580,000. Under ASC 323-10-15-13, how should Piedmont account for the transition?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Remeasure the original 10% to fair value, recognize a $30,000 gain in net income, and apply the equity method to the combined 25% interest prospectively from the step-acquisition date", "Carry the original 10% at $150,000 cost and apply the equity method only to the new 15%", "Restate all prior periods as if the equity method had been applied from inception", "Recognize a $430,000 gain representing the difference between total fair value and original cost"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 323-10-15-13, when an investor obtains significant influence through a step acquisition, the existing investment is remeasured to fair value. Since the 10% was already at $180,000 carrying amount (fair value under ASC 321), the remeasurement gain would reflect any difference. The equity method applies prospectively to the combined interest. Wrong (B): Applying the equity method only to the incremental shares violates ASC 323, which requires the entire investment to be accounted for under the equity method. Wrong (C): ASC 323 does not require retrospective restatement; the equity method is applied from the date significant influence is obtained. Wrong (D): $430,000 incorrectly compares total fair value to original cost without considering the two separate transactions and carrying amounts.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Equity method losses exceeding investment plus advances
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Oakdale Corp. owns 30% of Riverbend LLC with an investment carrying amount of $120,000 and a $40,000 loan receivable from Riverbend. Riverbend reports a net loss of $700,000 for 2026. What is the effect on Oakdale's financial statements?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Recognize a $210,000 loss reducing the investment to zero", "Recognize a $160,000 loss, reducing the investment and loan to zero, and discontinue recognizing further losses", "Recognize the full $210,000 loss even though it exceeds the carrying amounts", "Recognize a $120,000 loss reducing the investment to zero with no effect on the loan"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 323-10-35-19, Oakdale's share of the loss is 30% × $700,000 = $210,000. Losses first reduce the investment ($120,000), then other investments in the investee including loans ($40,000), totaling $160,000. The remaining $50,000 is not recognized because the investor has no further obligation. Wrong (A): $210,000 does not account for the fact that losses are capped at the total exposure including loans. Wrong (C): Recognizing the full $210,000 would create a negative investment balance, which is not permitted absent a guarantee or funding commitment. Wrong (D): Under ASC 323-10-35-19, loans and advances to the investee are also reduced after the investment reaches zero.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Measurement alternative under ASC 321
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Under ASC 321, what is the measurement alternative for equity securities without a readily determinable fair value?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Amortized cost less impairment", "Lower of cost or net realizable value", "Cost minus impairment, adjusted for observable price changes", "Fair value reported through other comprehensive income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 321-10-35-2, the measurement alternative allows equity securities without readily determinable fair values to be measured at cost, minus impairment, plus or minus changes resulting from observable price changes in orderly transactions. Wrong (A): Amortized cost less impairment applies to held-to-maturity debt securities under ASC 320, not equity securities under ASC 321. Wrong (B): Lower of cost or net realizable value was eliminated for equity securities by ASU 2016-01. Wrong (D): Fair value through OCI applies to certain AFS debt securities under ASC 320, not equity securities.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Trading securities gain/loss on sale
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Foxworth Trading bought an equity security classified as trading for $45,000 on June 1, 2026. At September 30, the security's fair value was $48,200. On November 15, Foxworth sold the security for $51,000. What gain does Foxworth report in the fourth quarter on the sale?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$6,000 total gain from cost", "$3,200 prior unrealized gain", "$2,800 gain at fair value", "$2,800 fourth-quarter gain"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 321-10-35-1, trading securities are carried at fair value. At September 30, a $3,200 unrealized gain was already recognized. The fourth-quarter gain on sale is $51,000 − $48,200 = $2,800, since the carrying amount was adjusted to fair value at the previous reporting date. Wrong (A): $6,000 represents the total gain from original cost, ignoring the $3,200 already recognized in Q3. Wrong (B): $3,200 was the unrealized gain recognized in the prior quarter, not the current quarter's gain. Wrong (C): $2,800 at fair value describes the measurement basis, not the realized gain on sale in the fourth quarter.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Transaction costs for equity securities at fair value
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Under ASC 321, how are transaction costs treated when purchasing equity securities measured at fair value through net income?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Expensed as incurred", "Capitalized as part of the investment cost and amortized", "Included in other comprehensive income", "Deferred and recognized upon sale of the security"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 321-10-25, transaction costs for equity securities measured at fair value through net income are expensed as incurred because the investment is subsequently remeasured to fair value each period, and including costs in the basis would be immediately offset. Wrong (B): Capitalizing and amortizing transaction costs applies to held-to-maturity debt securities, not equity securities at fair value. Wrong (C): Transaction costs are not an OCI item; they are recognized in earnings. Wrong (D): Deferring costs until sale is inconsistent with the fair value measurement model for equity securities.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Equity method investment balance after multiple transactions
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Thornbury Corp. owns 30% of Waverly Partners. The investment balance at January 1, 2026, is $540,000. During 2026, Waverly reports net income of $400,000 and declares dividends of $100,000. What is Thornbury's investment balance at December 31, 2026?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$540,000", "$630,000", "$660,000", "$720,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 323-10-35-4, the equity method investment increases by Thornbury's share of income (30% × $400,000 = $120,000) and decreases by dividends received (30% × $100,000 = $30,000). Balance: $540,000 + $120,000 − $30,000 = $630,000. Wrong (A): $540,000 ignores both the income and dividend adjustments required under the equity method. Wrong (C): $660,000 adds the full income share but fails to subtract the dividend received. Wrong (D): $720,000 incorrectly adds both income and dividends instead of subtracting dividends.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Consolidation requirement ownership threshold
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Under ASC 810, at what ownership level does a parent generally consolidate a subsidiary through a voting interest model?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Ownership of 20% or more", "Exactly 50% of voting shares", "More than 50% of voting interest", "Any ownership with board representation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 810-10-15-8, a parent with a controlling financial interest, generally defined as more than 50% of the outstanding voting shares, must consolidate the subsidiary. Wrong (A): Ownership of 20% or more creates a presumption of significant influence for the equity method under ASC 323, not consolidation. Wrong (B): Exactly 50% of voting shares does not constitute a controlling interest; it represents joint control at best. Wrong (D): Any ownership with board representation may indicate significant influence but does not trigger mandatory consolidation without majority voting control.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Investee stock issuance diluting investor ownership
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Bellamy Corp. owns 30% of Juniper Inc., carrying the investment at $900,000. Juniper issues additional shares to outside investors at $40 per share, diluting Bellamy's ownership to 22%. Bellamy's proportionate share of Juniper's net assets was $850,000 before the issuance and $780,000 after. What is the most likely effect on Bellamy's financial statements?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No accounting effect because Bellamy still uses the equity method", "A gain of $50,000 recognized in net income", "A loss of $120,000 recognized in net income", "A loss of $70,000 recognized in net income reflecting the dilution of Bellamy's share of net assets"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 323-10-40-1, when an investee issues stock that dilutes the investor's ownership, the investor adjusts the carrying amount to reflect the change in its share of net assets. Bellamy's share dropped from $850,000 to $780,000, a $70,000 reduction recognized as a loss. Wrong (A): Dilution from investee stock issuances requires recognition of a gain or loss; it is not ignored. Wrong (B): $50,000 does not correspond to the actual change in proportionate net assets. Wrong (C): $120,000 incorrectly calculates the difference between carrying amount and the new share of net assets without properly isolating the dilution effect.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — AFS debt securities unrealized gains reporting location
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Under ASC 320, where are unrealized holding gains on available-for-sale debt securities initially reported?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Accumulated other comprehensive income on the balance sheet", "Interest income on the income statement", "Retained earnings directly", "A valuation allowance contra account"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 320-10-35-1, unrealized gains and losses on AFS debt securities are excluded from earnings and reported in other comprehensive income, accumulating in AOCI on the balance sheet. Wrong (B): Interest income reflects coupon and amortization amounts, not fair value changes. Wrong (C): Unrealized gains flow through OCI, not directly to retained earnings. Wrong (D): AFS fair value adjustments are reported in AOCI, not through a valuation allowance contra account.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — HTM bond discount accretion carrying amount
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Sentinel Insurance purchased a held-to-maturity bond on January 1, 2026, for $94,200 with a face value of $100,000, a 4% stated rate, and a 5.2% effective yield. What is the carrying amount of the bond on December 31, 2026?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$94,200", "$95,098", "$95,320", "$100,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 320-10-35-1, interest income is $94,200 × 5.2% = $4,898. Cash received is $100,000 × 4% = $4,000. Discount accretion is $4,898 − $4,000 = $898, bringing the carrying amount to $94,200 + $898 = $95,098. Wrong (A): $94,200 is the purchase price; discount accretion increases the carrying amount each period. Wrong (C): $95,320 likely results from applying the effective rate to face value instead of carrying amount. Wrong (D): $100,000 is the face value the bond will reach at maturity, not after one year of accretion.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Observable price change adjustment under measurement alternative
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Under ASC 321-10-35-2, what triggers an upward adjustment for an equity security carried under the measurement alternative?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A management estimate of increased intrinsic value", "A favorable analyst report on the investee", "An observable price change in an orderly transaction for an identical or similar security", "An increase in the investee's reported net income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 321-10-35-2, upward adjustments to equity securities under the measurement alternative are permitted only when there is an observable price change from an orderly transaction for the identical or a similar security of the same issuer. Wrong (A): Management estimates alone do not qualify as observable transactions under the standard. Wrong (B): Analyst reports are opinions, not observable price changes in actual transactions. Wrong (D): Increases in investee net income do not trigger measurement alternative adjustments; only observable market transactions qualify.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — Equity method losses suspended at zero basis
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Drayton Holdings owns 35% of Capstone LLC with an investment carrying amount of $50,000 and no loans or guarantees. Capstone reports a net loss of $200,000 in 2026 and net income of $100,000 in 2027. How should Drayton account for the 2027 income?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Recognize $35,000 equity income immediately", "Recognize $35,000 equity income only after recovering the $20,000 of unrecognized losses", "Recognize no equity income until Capstone's cumulative income exceeds cumulative losses", "Recognize $15,000 equity income ($35,000 less the $20,000 suspended loss)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Drayton's 2026 share of loss is 35% × $200,000 = $70,000, but recognition stops at $50,000 (carrying amount), suspending $20,000. In 2027, Drayton's share of income is 35% × $100,000 = $35,000. Under ASC 323-10-35-19, the suspended losses must be recovered first: $35,000 − $20,000 = $15,000 recognized as equity income. Wrong (A): Recognizing the full $35,000 ignores the $20,000 of previously suspended losses that must be absorbed first. Wrong (B): This describes the correct concept but states income is delayed entirely until recovery, when in fact net income recognition begins in the same period. Wrong (C): This overly restrictive approach is not required; recovery occurs as the investor's share of subsequent income exceeds suspended losses.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: hard / L4 — Evaluate AFS impairment credit vs non-credit
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Clearwater Bank holds an AFS corporate bond with amortized cost of $500,000 and fair value of $440,000. The issuer recently lost a major customer representing 30% of revenue, and Clearwater estimates it will collect only $475,000 of contractual cash flows. Clearwater intends to hold the bond until recovery. Under ASC 326-30, evaluate the appropriate accounting treatment.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Record a $25,000 credit loss allowance in net income and $35,000 non-credit loss in OCI", "Record the full $60,000 decline as a credit loss in net income", "Record nothing because Clearwater intends to hold until recovery", "Record a $60,000 loss in OCI because intent to hold prevents income statement recognition"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 326-30-35-3, the credit loss is the difference between amortized cost and expected cash flows: $500,000 − $475,000 = $25,000, recognized as an allowance charged to net income. The remaining decline ($60,000 − $25,000 = $35,000) is non-credit and reported in OCI. Wrong (B): Only the credit-related portion flows through net income; the non-credit portion goes to OCI. Wrong (C): Under ASC 326, intent to hold does not eliminate the requirement to recognize credit losses. Wrong (D): The credit component must be separated and recognized in income regardless of intent to hold.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q26: medium / L3 — Reclassification prohibition from trading category
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Mercer Capital classified a debt security as trading upon purchase. Six months later, market conditions deteriorate and Mercer's management wants to reclassify the security to held-to-maturity to avoid further income statement volatility. Under ASC 320, what is the most likely outcome?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The transfer is permitted if Mercer demonstrates positive intent and ability to hold", "The transfer is permitted at fair value with prior unrealized gains or losses remaining in retained earnings", "The transfer requires SEC approval for publicly traded entities", "Transfers from trading to any other category should be rare and are strongly discouraged"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 320-10-35-10, transfers from trading are permitted but should be rare. The security transfers at fair value, which becomes the new amortized cost basis. Any unrealized gain or loss already recognized in earnings is not reversed. Wrong (A): Demonstrating intent and ability is necessary for HTM classification but does not independently authorize the transfer; the rarity condition also applies. Wrong (C): SEC approval is not a requirement for reclassification under ASC 320. Wrong (D): While transfers from trading should be rare, the standard does not prohibit them; it permits transfers at fair value under specific conditions.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Dividends received under equity method
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Lambert Corp. owns 25% of Sterling Partners. Sterling declares and pays dividends of $200,000. Lambert's equity method investment balance before the dividend was $680,000. What is the carrying amount of Lambert's investment after receiving the dividend?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$680,000", "$730,000", "$630,000", "$480,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 323-10-35-4, dividends received from an equity method investee reduce the carrying amount of the investment. Lambert's share is 25% × $200,000 = $50,000, reducing the balance to $680,000 − $50,000 = $630,000. Dividends are not income under the equity method. Wrong (A): $680,000 ignores the dividend's effect on the investment balance. Wrong (B): $730,000 incorrectly adds the dividend to the investment balance; dividends reduce, not increase, the equity method carrying amount. Wrong (D): $480,000 incorrectly deducts the full $200,000 dividend rather than Lambert's 25% share.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Upstream equipment profit elimination
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Prescott Inc. owns 30% of Dalton Manufacturing. Dalton sold equipment with a book value of $120,000 to Prescott for $180,000. The equipment has a remaining useful life of 5 years with no salvage value. Under the equity method, what adjustment does Prescott record in the first year?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Reduce equity income by $60,000", "Reduce equity income by $18,000 and recognize $3,600 annual depreciation recovery", "Reduce equity income by $14,400", "Reduce equity income by $18,000 with a $3,600 annual recovery, net reduction of $14,400"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 323-10-35-7, for upstream sales the investor eliminates its share of intercompany profit. Unrealized profit is $180,000 − $120,000 = $60,000. Prescott's share is 30% × $60,000 = $18,000 deferred profit. Annual depreciation recovery is $18,000 ÷ 5 = $3,600. In year one, the net reduction to equity income is $18,000 − $3,600 = $14,400. Wrong (A): $60,000 is the total intercompany profit before applying the ownership percentage. Wrong (B): This identifies the components correctly but does not state the net effect. Wrong (C): $14,400 is the correct net amount but does not explain the two components, making it incomplete as a description of the adjustment.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Fair value through net income equity securities calculation
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Brookfield Asset Management purchased 5,000 shares of a publicly traded company at $24 per share on March 1, 2026. At June 30, the shares trade at $27. At September 30, the shares trade at $22. What is the total unrealized loss recognized through the first three quarters of 2026?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$10,000 unrealized loss", "No loss because the security was above cost at June 30", "$15,000 unrealized loss recognized in Q3 only", "$25,000 unrealized loss"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 321-10-35-1, equity securities are measured at fair value through net income each period. Cost was 5,000 × $24 = $120,000. At September 30, fair value is 5,000 × $22 = $110,000. Cumulative unrealized loss is $120,000 − $110,000 = $10,000 through the first three quarters. Wrong (B): Interim gains do not offset the cumulative measurement; the net position from cost to current fair value is a $10,000 loss. Wrong (C): $15,000 measures only the Q3 decline ($27 to $22 × 5,000) and ignores the Q2 gain already recognized. Wrong (D): $25,000 has no relationship to the actual price changes in this scenario.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Hedge designation for AFS debt securities
(2, 'Investments', $EXPL$Ridgeline Financial holds a portfolio of AFS fixed-rate corporate bonds exposed to interest rate risk. Management wants to designate a fair value hedge using interest rate swaps to reduce income statement volatility from potential reclassifications upon sale. Under ASC 815-20-25, which factor is most critical for the hedge to qualify?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The bonds must be government-issued securities", "The hedging relationship must be formally documented at inception with specified effectiveness testing methodology", "The swap must have the exact same notional amount and maturity as the bonds", "Hedge accounting is not available for AFS debt securities under any circumstances"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 815-20-25-1, formal documentation at inception is the critical qualifying requirement. Documentation must specify the hedging instrument, hedged item, risk being hedged, and how effectiveness will be assessed. Without it, hedge accounting cannot be applied. Wrong (A): ASC 815 does not restrict hedged items to government securities; any qualifying financial asset can be hedged. Wrong (C): While matching terms improves effectiveness, exact matching is not an absolute requirement; the critical requirement is formal documentation and effectiveness testing. Wrong (D): Fair value hedges are explicitly available for AFS debt securities under ASC 815-20-25 to hedge changes in fair value due to interest rate risk.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;